      "latency_ms": 3072.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 707,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than just a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it matters, and how it behaves:\n\n### 1. The Unique Physical Properties of Water\nWater is famous for being utterly bizarre compared to other liquids on Earth. Its unique properties make life possible:\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (oceans, rivers), **solid** (ice, snow), and **gas** (steam, vapor).\n* **Universal Solvent:** Water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances. Because of its chemical structure, it easily bonds with and carries minerals, nutrients, and chemicals, which is why it is called the \"universal solvent.\"\n* **Density Anomaly:** Almost all liquids shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns into ice. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying all aquatic life.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a massive amount of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps our body temperatures stable.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater is the defining feature of our home planet. \n* **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly by oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Despite all that water, only about **2.5% is fresh water** (needed for drinking and agriculture). Even worse, over two-thirds of that fresh water is locked away in glaciers and polar ice caps, leaving less than 1% easily accessible to humans in lakes, rivers, and underground aquifers.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly recycled through the hydrologic cycle: it evaporates from the oceans, forms clouds, falls as rain or snow (precipitation), and flows back to the sea via rivers. The water you drink today is the exact same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago!\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nBiology cannot exist without water. \n* **The Human Body:** An adult human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. Our brains are about 73% water, our lungs about 83%, and even our bones are roughly 31% water.\n* **Cellular Function:** Water acts as the internal fluid environment for all cells. It flushes out toxins, cushions joints, regulates body temperature through sweat, and transports nutrients through the bloodstream.\n* **Origin of Life:** Scientists believe that life itself originated in Earth's primordial oceans roughly 3.8 billion years ago, relying on water's ability to facilitate complex chemical reactions.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is the lifeblood of planet Earth. It is a simple molecule with extraordinary powers—shaping our geology, regulating our climate, and making the existence of living creatures possible."
